-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Memory Constraints**: The Python/PyTorch loop implementation is slow and memory-intensive for large $N$ [S2, unofficial]. The roadmap indicates a transition to CUDA kernels for real-time performance [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Covariance Singularity**: If the 2D covariance matrix is singular, `torch.inverse` will fail [G]. A small epsilon should be added to the diagonal [G].
-*   **Alpha Clamping**: Alpha values are clamped to a maximum of 0.99 to prevent division by zero in transmittance updates and ensure numerical stability [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Sorting Order**: Incorrect sorting (e.g., front-to-back) will result in incorrect alpha compositing and visual artifacts [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Performance**: Not suitable for real-time rendering or large-scale scenes without CUDA optimization [S2, unofficial].
+*   `scale_modifier`: Multiplicative scalar applied to Gaussian scales during rasterization. Default value in `render_view` is `1.0` [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Pitfalls
+*   **Memory Constraints**: The Python/PyTorch loop implementation iterates over all Gaussians, which is slow and memory-intensive for large $N$ [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Precision**: Clamping alpha to a maximum of `0.99` prevents numerical instability in transmittance [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Input Validation**: Failing to provide exactly one of `shs`/`colors_precomp` or `scales`/`rotations`/`cov3D_precomp` will raise an exception in the CUDA wrapper [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Analytic-Splatting](3025-analytic-splatting.md)
-Analytic-Splatting is a differentiable rasterization method that replaces the point-sampling of 2D Gaussians in 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) with an analytical approximation of the integral of the Gaussian signal over the pixel window area. It solves the aliasing artifacts (blurring or jaggies) caused by treating pixels as isolated points when the camera zooms or resolution changes. Unlike [Mip-Splatting](3415-gaussian-splatting-rendering-pipeline.md) which uses a hybrid low-pass filter that suppresses high-frequency components and causes over-smoothing, Analytic-Splatting preserves high-quality details by calculating the integral analytically using a conditioned logistic function approximation of the Gaussian CDF.
+Analytic-Splatting replaces the point-sampling of 2D Gaussians in 3D Gaussian Splatting with an analytical approximation of the integral of the Gaussian signal over the pixel window area to reduce aliasing artifacts during zooming or resolution changes. It addresses the limitation of standard 3DGS, which treats pixels as isolated points and suffers from aliasing when pixel footprints change due to focal length or camera distance adjustments. Unlike Mip-Splatting, which uses a hybrid filtering mechanism that suppresses high-frequency components and causes over-smoothing, Analytic-Splatting preserves high-quality details by analytically integrating the signal using a conditioned logistic function approximation of the Gaussian CDF.
 
